
HYPOTHESIS (data-grounded, NLSY79 1979 snapshot; n=8467 composite-SES complete-case)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
How is the advantage in BA degree completion rates for Black students related to SES levels?

The Black-vs-NON-BLACK-NON-HISPANIC gap in BA-completion-relevant educational attainment
(highest grade completed >= 16 as of 1979; corroborated by continuous grade attainment and
associate+ (>=14) thresholds) is NEGATIVELY related to SES level: the Black shortfall is
concentrated at the LOW-SES tercile and is essentially eliminated - or replaced by a small
Black lead - at middle/high SES.

(a) Continuous attainment (age-adjusted ANCOVA, hgc ~ race*SES + age):
    Black - NBNH gap: Low = -0.40 grade, Mid = +0.08, High = -0.11 grade;
    race x SES interaction p = 4.6e-09 (robust to age-quadratic, imputation+missing indicators,
    and full confounder model incl. sex/ASVAB/familysize/jail p=0.006-0.009).
    => Black shortfall is largest at low SES and vanishes at mid/high SES.

(b) BA (HGC>=16) completion BY 1979 (age-censored; low power, exact tests only):
    Low-SES:    Black 0.13% vs NBNH 0.31%  (gap -0.18pp, RR=0.41, Fisher p=0.66)
    Mid-SES:    Black 0.00% vs NBNH 0.17% (gap -0.17pp, RR=1.07, Fisher p=1.00)
    High-SES:   Black 1.35% vs NBNH 0.54% (gap +0.81pp, RR=2.50, Fisher p=0.15)
    => direction switches from Black below (low/mid SES) to Black above (high SES);
       none individually significant at the =16 threshold (rare events).

(c) Age-restricted (age>=20, i.e. BA-by-1979 plausible): Low-SES gap -0.42pp,
    High-SES gap +1.66pp (Black 3.39% vs NBNH 1.73%) - same reversal.

(d) Associate+ (>=14) threshold (main composite SES): NBNH-Black gap narrows monotonically with
    SES: 2.74pp (Low) -> 2.17pp (Mid) -> 0.90pp (High), i.e. higher SES closes the gap.

Interpretation: the size of the Black 'advantage' in BA degree completion is SES-dependent -
there is no invariant Black advantage; instead the racial gap is inversely related to SES:
the largest Black disadvantage occurs at LOW SES and higher SES levels are associated with
parity or a small relative Black advantage in BA completion by 1979.

Major limitations: education measured ONLY in the 1979 interview (respondents aged 14-22), so
'BA completion' here means completion by ~1979 (heavily age-censored; effectively only the
age>=20 tail). Only 37 BA>=16 events overall (26 in complete-case),
cells 0-9 events -> Fisher-exact only, no multivariate BA-regression. Complete-case retains
only 70% of BA events (differential by race). Results at the exact BA threshold are not
statistically significant; the strongest evidence comes from continuous attainment and >=14
thresholds. ASVAB/preparation adjustment erases the raw racial gap (prep largely explains it).
